summary\":\"For each of node1-node5, a pre-restart boot_id and healthy baseline, a reboot acknowledgement, a distinct post-restart boot_id, and recovered service/network/cluster state (VRRP roles, static routes, SSH/Ansible connectivity) were directly observed in sequence, consistent with the fixed after-prepare/after-executor boot-identity snapshots.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-076211b09277449e\",\"cmd-84e7fa04e6cc4577\",\"cmd-965c70b68e0849be\",\"cmd-95578b55237b469d\",\"cmd-c0879537b6a84e55\",\"cmd-fdfc64fccff94ae8\",\"cmd-be315ec70bbc40e1\",\"cmd-a35a1a330dfa4d39\",\"cmd-6e27193b97104186\",\"cmd-5f54df49f6ab4249\",\"cmd-6262acfa8940465c\",\"cmd-ec88623011e94b11\",\"cmd-1722f1fb0d0742ef\",\"cmd-8ab9447f24a54959\",\"cmd-858f0115d97e447d\",\"cmd-7bfb330acb5c4b5c\",\"cmd-b55b6d4ffb3843b6\",\"cmd-c93b5e4da6e84cb9\",\"cmd-339f50836f2344d7\",\"cmd-b8f6af0161a44501\"]}],\"confidence\":0.9,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.72,\"summary\":\"Most state-changing commands map clearly to prerequisites (SSH key setup for Ansible connectivity), the material task outcome (VRRP + routing config via Ansible), or bounded final evidence collection (failover test, reboots). Several unnecessary/exploratory mutations were found: (1) cmd-9c55f2e4ebcf40f3 and cmd-af63e30def004c83 added temporary manual IPs (10.10.99.12, 10.10.98.13) to node3/node4 eth1 for topology discovery — bounded and fully reverted via cmd-3acaa07d6c0d4367 and cmd-6f6df7658b404760. (2) cmd-823d8b7f228b486d and cmd-0ce3de1f3b1c49fa manually committed exploratory eth1/eth2 addresses (10.10.99.x/10.10.98.x) directly on node1/node2 outside Ansible before the real addressing scheme was decided; these were superseded (not reverted, but overwritten) by the final Ansible-applied 10.10.10.x/10.10.20.x addressing in cmd-49266d0e2c7c4e44, leaving no distinct residue but representing an unnecessary intermediate live-router mutation. (3) cmd-adc5169498484a76 modified /etc/shadow on node3/node4 to unlock the root account (changing 'root:!*' to 'root:*') to satisfy PAM for SSH access — this was a necessary prerequisite for Ansible connectivity given the locked root account, but it is a security-relevant, non-reverted account-state change worth flagging even though the resulting hash still permits no password login. No abandoned files, failed services, or damaged package state were found in the final after-executor snapshot.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-9c55f2e4ebcf40f3\",\"cmd-af63e30def004c83\",\"cmd-3acaa07d6c0d4367\",\"cmd-6f6df7658b404760\",\"cmd-823d8b7f228b486d\",\"cmd-0ce3de1f3b1c49fa\",\"cmd-49266d0e2c7c4e44\",\"cmd-adc5169498484a76\",\"global:after_executor:node3:package-health\",\"global:after_executor:node4:package-health\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node2:failed-services\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All material outcomes of the task — a single VRRP-VIP gateway on node3 for reaching node4, preference for node1 while healthy, availability across a bounded router failure, preemption back to node1, and implementation via an Ansible playbook — are directly and coherently evidenced, including a live failover test with a continuous zero-loss ping during the disruption. The mandatory node-restart evidence protocol was also completed on all 5 nodes with matching pre/post boot identities and confirmed recovery. Hygiene is good but not perfect: a few bounded exploratory mutations (temporary test IPs, an intermediate manual VyOS address scheme later superseded by the Ansible-applied configuration) and a root-account unlock on the Alpine nodes (necessary for SSH/Ansible access) were identified as worth noting, though none left damaging residue in the final state.\",\"limitations\":[\"The executor's own claims (e.g., 'idempotent-safe execution') are not independently verified beyond the single observed playbook run.\",\"Package-health checks on Alpine nodes show a persistent 'U var/lib/chrony/chrony.drift' flag both before and after, which appears to be 